WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:08.180
ZOS isn't the only operating system you'll find in an enterprise environment.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:13.300
Over the years, Unix has also become widely used in a variety of industries.

3
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.500
It was so popular that a bunch of people who wanted their own version of Unix went out

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.500
and made Linux.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.500
Now, the evolution of Unix and the history of Linux is pretty interesting, and it's

6
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.540
a fun research rabbit hole to go down, but the guys behind the camera are giving me a

7
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.460
look right now, so I better get back on topic.

8
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.540
Today, Linux is everywhere, but before Linux really took off, people in mainframe shops

9
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.140
really wanted a way to use Unix.

10
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:43.700
So the operating system was enhanced with Unix System Services, or USS.

11
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:50.580
Now, point of clarification, USS is not a virtual or an emulated operating system.

12
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:56.860
It's an implementation of a Unix environment, so you can use commands like ls, grep, cat,

13
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.540
and makedir, and you can even write and run shell scripts without leaving zos.

14
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.140
Pretty neat trick.

15
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.860
And again, because it's an implementation, it's using the same APIs behind the scenes

16
00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:14.540
that zos does, so it's not like going into USS gobbles up another operating system's

17
00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.420
worth of resources.

18
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.840
And it's not just there in case you feel like typing Unix commands.

19
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.140
For some zos middleware, you'll find installation, configuration, and automation utilities implemented

20
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.940
using USS.

21
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.740
And for someone with a Unix Linux background, like myself, I like being able to script and

22
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.980
hack away at shell scripts.

23
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.420
Before we move on, here's an easy one just to make sure we're all good on the basics.

24
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.460
Fill in the blank.

25
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.540
Unix System Services on zos is a what?

26
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:52.100
Implementation, virtual operating system, hypervisor, or Linux kernel module.

27
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.140
I hope you picked implementation, and I'm going to keep driving that point home because

28
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.980
it's important to remember that USS is just that, an implementation.

29
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.240
It's not a separate operating system, or a virtualized operating system, or a remote

30
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.280
system, or anything like that.

31
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.760
Okay, now there's two main ways of driving USS.

32
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:17.400
The first is the interactive mode, which you can get to with the tso command omvs.

33
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.460
That gives you a shell, which is how you can enter commands and see output.

34
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.940
You can also use the i shell, which gives you a panel driven interface into USS.

35
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.820
Behind the scenes, it's all the same API, so it really comes down to a matter of taste.

36
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:37.520
Even between zos and uss, it's not an all one or the other type thing.

37
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.700
You can have one interacting with the other.

38
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.900
For example, you can move files from one environment to the other.

39
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.240
You can issue tso commands from the shell command line.

40
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.820
You can interact with Unix files through the tso commands.

41
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.380
You can even write JCL scripts that include shell commands.

42
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.020
It's all intertwined.

43
00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.940
Here's what the omvs shell looks like.

44
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.500
There's a command line, there's the function keys, and over here on the right, the status

45
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.740
indicator.

46
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:08.580
The status indicator will go back and forth between input and running, depending on if

47
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.060
it's waiting for you, or you're waiting for it.

48
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.740
Working with directories and files is one of the first things you'll need to do in Unix,

49
00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.140
and there's a whole lot of commands that you'll use to make that happen.

50
00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:26.860
When you want to make a directory, which is just another word for folder, use mkdir, short

51
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.700
for make directory.

52
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.220
Then cd is used to change the directory.

53
00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.920
So after you've made a directory, you can use cd to go into it.

54
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.540
And then you can use ls, the list command, to look around that directory.

55
00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.500
And if you want to move files back and forth between a Unix file system and zos, then check

56
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.140
out the oput and oget commands.

57
00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.020
That's basically the file system structure of a Unix system.

58
00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.220
You've got the file system, and you can fill it with files and directories, and within

59
00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.780
those directories, you can put even more files and directories, and so on.

60
00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.180
And a file can be something that you can read.

61
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.220
It can also be an executable program.

62
00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.940
And there's other types of things that you can put in a directory as well, but files

63
00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.540
and folders is the main name of the game.

64
00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.940
Now I could stand here and list Unix commands for hours, but I don't think that would be

65
00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.820
a good use of either of our times.

66
00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.340
Suffice to say, lots of commands.

67
00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.980
And you won't find every command that you would expect to find on a Linux system, but

68
00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.660
it's still a good amount.

69
00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:33.980
And if you really want to run Linux, there are plenty of ways of running Linux on z.

70
00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:39.280
Unix system services can use a type of file system called zfs, and one interesting thing

71
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.440
about zfs is that it's sysplex aware, and you'll learn a little bit more about that

72
00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.200
from my friend Louisa in a few minutes.

73
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.360
You can also mount a file system over a network.

74
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.200
This is called NFS, or network file system.

75
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.040
These network file systems can be mainframe to mainframe, or even mainframe to desktop

76
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.200
or desktop to mainframe.

77
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.400
NFS is the commonality that makes all of that possible.

78
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.120
And lastly, there's the TFS.

79
00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.580
This is the temporary file system.

80
00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:13.860
It's an in-memory physical file system, and as the name suggests, it's a good place for

81
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.420
writing up files that you only need for a short amount of time.

82
00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:21.660
If you need those files to be there tomorrow or next week, it's probably a good idea to

83
00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.300
make sure those get copied somewhere more permanent when you're done for the day.

84
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.020
And since we're done for the day, let's close this up and get on to the next topic.
